--- body ---
## Context

`tickets/erg-github` is, in the README's own words (L104-105), "a separate
committed POSIX-sh helper (**it travels with the clone**)". Unlike `.ergrc`
and `AGENTS.md`, it is not an embedded asset: it is vendored into each
adopter repo as a plain committed file, and nothing in the toolchain ever
compares an adopter's copy against the shipped one.

That means it has **no staleness channel at all**. The asset machinery —
`.erg-assets`, `isCleanUpgrade`, `assetDriftWarnings`, the whole 3-state
dpkg compare that 0278, 0279 and 0281 exist to harden — does not cover this
file. An adopter running a year-old `erg-github` gets no warning, no drift
signal, and no `erg check` complaint.

The concrete bite: 0255 fixes a ticket-ID extraction gap in
`tickets/erg-github`'s `cmd_verify()`. 0255's own Reproduction section names
`aedist-technical-report` PR #442/#443 as where that bug actually fired —
and that repo has its own committed copy of the buggy script. Merging 0255
into git-erg fixes git-erg's copy and reaches nothing else. Every adopter
needs a manual re-vendor that nothing will ever prompt them to do.

Note the shape: this is the same defect class the asset-machinery tickets
are about — a tracked file drifting silently — applied to the one file none
of them covers. 0278's own invariant ("no tracked file is ever overwritten
or reverted without saying so") has a sibling this repo has never stated:
*no shipped file goes stale in an adopter without saying so.*

## Relevant files

- `tickets/erg-github` — the vendored helper itself
- `README.md` L100-108 — where the "travels with the clone" contract is stated
- `src/go/init.go` — `initAssetPaths`, `migrateAssetPaths`, `orphanAssetPaths`;
  `erg-github` appears in none of them
- `src/go/manifest.go` — the drift machinery that does not see this file
- `src/go/check.go` — where a staleness report would most naturally surface

## Actions

1. Decide the contract first, because the rest follows from it, and this is
   an architectural call, not an implementation detail. Three coherent
   options, and they are genuinely different products:
   a. **Make it an embedded asset** like `.ergrc`/`AGENTS.md` — `erg init`
      installs and upgrades it through the existing 3-state compare. Adopters
      converge automatically. Costs: the forge layer stops being optional,
      and a local edit to it now needs the same divergence handling.
   b. **Keep it vendored, add a version stamp** — `erg-github` carries a
      version line, `erg check` compares it against the running binary's
      expectation and reports staleness without touching the file. Cheapest
      honest fix: it makes the drift *visible* without claiming ownership of
      the file.
   c. **Declare it explicitly out of scope** — document in README that
      `erg-github` is a starting point adopters own outright, and that
      upstream fixes must be pulled manually. Costs nothing to build, but
      the cost lands on every adopter forever, silently.
2. Implement whichever the author picks.
3. Regardless of choice: record in 0255's wake which adopters are known to
   carry a stale copy. `aedist-technical-report` is one, confirmed by 0255's
   own reproduction.

## Test

If (a): the existing asset tests extend to cover `erg-github` — the same
fixtures 0281 builds.
If (b): a fixture repo with an old `erg-github` must produce a *visible*
staleness message from `erg check`, asserted on content, not exit code —
the same discipline 0281 uses for path A, because the defect here is also
silence.
If (c): a docs test asserting the README states the manual-pull contract.

## Verification

- [ ] the contract is decided and written down somewhere durable, not just
      in this ticket
- [ ] an adopter carrying a stale `erg-github` either converges (a), is told
      (b), or is documented as owning the problem (c)
- [ ] the known-stale adopters from Action 3 are listed

## Invariants

- `erg` core stays offline and forge-blind. Whatever is chosen here must not
  make the binary reach the network or depend on `gh`.
- A non-forge adopter is never wedged by this: `erg-github` is optional
  infrastructure and must remain so.

## Exit criteria

- The vendoring contract for `tickets/erg-github` is explicit, and drift in
  an adopter copy is either impossible, visible, or documented as the
  adopter's own responsibility

## Related

- 0255 — the fix whose reach this ticket is about. 0255 lands in git-erg's
  copy only; that is deliberate and does not wait on this ticket.
- 0278, 0281 — the same "a tracked file drifts silently" class, for the
  embedded assets that *do* have a staleness channel.
